     3,
     "\r<br>\r<br>On Thu, 2024-02-15 at 10:59 -0800, Prince Michael Amegbor wrote:\r<br>&gt; Hi everyone,\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; I&#39;m exploring the implementation of a modified Poisson regression with\r<br>&gt; robust variance, following the recommendation by Zhou et al. in 2004.\r<br>&gt; This is particularly useful for modeling a binary outcome with a\r<br>&gt; common prevalence ratio (PR).\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; In R, you can achieve this using the glm model and the sandwich\r<br>&gt; package for robust variance. Here&#39;s an example:\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; library(&quot;sandwich&quot;)\r<br>&gt; library(&quot;lmtest&quot;)\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; model &lt;- glm(outcome ~ exposure, family = poisson(link = log), data =\r<br>&gt; dataset)\r<br>&gt; coeftest(model, vcov = sandwich)\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; This model, expressed as log(λ) = β₀ + β₁ ⋅ exposure, calculates\r<br>&gt; standard errors with robust variance for more reliable results.\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; SErobust​(βi​)=V(βi​)√​\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; I&#39;m curious if there&#39;s a way to implement this approach in the r-inla.\r<br>&gt;  \r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; \r<br>"
    ],
